Bandit12:
  Converting the file from hexdump to regular and then using file command to know what kind of file we're dealing with brings us that:
```
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ ls
data.txt
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ xxd -r data.txt > data
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ ls
data  data.txt
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ file data
data: gzip compressed data, was "data2.bin", last modified: Tue Oct 14 09:26:06 2025, max compression, from Unix, original size modulo 2^32 564
```

Now since we can't unzip the file immediately. We need to rename it so that the gzip tool can recognise it:
```
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ gzip -d data
gzip: data: unknown suffix -- ignored
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ mv data data.gz
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ mv data data.gz
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ ls
data.gz  data.txt
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ gzip -d data.gz
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ ls
data  data.txt
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ file data
data: bzip2 compressed data, block size = 900k

```

A few more rounds of decompressing and renaming and decompressing and decompressing and decom-I am sorry, this is just frustrating:
```
bandit12@bandit:/tmp/dir852369$ cat data8
The password is FO5dwFsc0cbaIiH0h8J2eUks2vdTDwAn
```

